+buffer of `n` bytes takes a granule of the smallest power of two that holds it — at least 16, with
+no upper limit — and its base is a multiple of that granule, which is the buffer's alone while it
+lives. `n` is the caller's to say: a heap block's is its `Layout` size, whatever the caller asks; a
+real array or typed array is its length times the element size of the first `addr` asked of it; and
+any other key — the object a field slot names, a record — is one element of that size, never a
+struct field that happens to be called `length`. A heap block's addresses therefore all lie inside
+its granule, so no address inside one live block equals an address inside another, and the
+comparisons that fall back to addresses across buffers — pointer `<` and `Ord`, and `as usize`
+values — order by block first and by offset within it, as Rust's disjoint allocations require. A
+non-heap buffer is only as large as its first `addr` says: asked first through an erased pointer
+(`*const ()`, whose element size is unknown and counted as one byte), an array of wider elements is
+given a granule too small for its later element addresses, and those can fall inside a neighbour's.
+The base is aligned for every type the buffer can hold, since a type's alignment divides its size.
+An alignment the size does not imply — a hand-built `Layout` whose alignment exceeds its size, or an
+over-aligned element first addressed through a byte pointer — is honoured only up to the buffer's
+granule (at least 16).
+
+A granule below 4096 bytes comes from a free list of its own size, else from a bump pointer starting
+at 4096, and — once the bump pointer has no room left — from a free large granule cut down to size;
+small granules are never merged. A granule of 4096 bytes or more comes from a buddy system: the
+smallest free granule that holds it, halved down to size with the unused halves left free, else the
+bump pointer; a freed one merges with its buddy for as long as the buddy is free at the same size,
+so the space a large buffer gives back serves a later large buffer of any size — a thousand 1 MiB
+blocks allocated and freed in cycles reuse the same megabytes. The alignment gap the bump pointer
+skips is split into free granules rather than lost, and the page at the zero-sized address is never
+anyone's.
+
+A granule comes back in four ways. `dealloc` gives a heap block's back — the freed buffer keeps its
+entry, so a dangling pointer still answers the address it had, and a later allocation may reuse it,
+as a native allocator would. A `realloc` that grows a block past its granule, at any size, gives it
+back too, and the block takes a granule of its new size when next asked: a reallocation may move in
